31: \begin{abstract}
32: Modern vehicles are software-defined, and each over-the-air update to a vehicle's AUTOSAR architecture can silently open a path from an attacker-reachable interface to a safety-critical actuator; existing diff-based review cannot tell whether such a path was created. We present \emph{arxml-secdiff}, a tool that parses two ARXML architecture snapshots into typed component-influence graphs,
<truncated 11844 bytes>
int to a safety-critical component, and gate the release accordingly. The challenge has three parts: (1)~\emph{semantics} --- deciding whether a connector is authenticated requires resolving the port$\to$data-element$\to$system-signal$\to$i-signal$\to$i-signal-i-pdu chain, and most connectors legitimately have no SecOC because they never leave an ECU; (2)~\emph{identity} --- two instances of one component type (e.g., four wheel sensors) must remain distinct nodes, or reachability ``teleports'' between siblings; (3)~\emph{honesty} --- criticality comes from a programme's HARA/TARA, not the ARXML, so guessed inputs must be marked as such, and an analysis that cannot answer the question must not masquerade as a clean bill of health.
