Provenance-Aware Risk Scoring}
\label{subsec:severity}

Findings are scored using the ISO/SAE~21434 Annex~H matrix~\cite{iso21434} (Table~\ref{tab:riskmatrix}), combining impact and feasibility into risk ratings $\{1,\ldots,5\}$.
Three core rules govern scoring:

\emph{ASIL-Derived Impact:}
Target safety impact is mapped directly from Automotive Safety Integrity Levels: $\text{ASIL-D} \to \text{severe}$, $\text{ASIL-C} \to \text{major}$, $\text{ASIL-B/A} \to \text{moderate}$, and $\text{QM} \to \text{negligible}$.
Unannotated critical assets default to major.

\emph{Downstream Impact Inheritance:}
Connector-level findings lacking explicit targets inherit the maximum impact of any safety-critical node reachable downstream along the path in the updated graph.
This enables unauthenticated intermediate hops to correctly reflect the criticality of actuators they indirectly influence.

\emph{Assumed Input Tainting:}
When roles or SecOC profiles are derived from heuristics rather than formal TARA configurations, findings receive an \texttt{assumed} taint.
Under default policies, assumed findings are downgraded from \textsc{Block} to \textsc{Flag} to avoid false-positive gate rejections.

Feasibility evaluates attack resistance.
Paths with unauthenticated bus hops or standalone missing-authentication findings are rated high feasibility.
Fully SecOC-protected paths are rated low feasibility, and intra-ECU paths are rated very low.
Remotely exposed surfaces (cellular, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth) increment base feasibility by one level up to high.

